# Packaging completeness for the shipped test suite.
#
# The sdist includes `tests/*.py` (setuptools picks them up), which meant an
# unpacked sdist shipped test files whose inputs it did not carry: no conftest,
# no `tests/fixtures/*.wasm`. Running `pytest` there produced dozens of failures
# that looked like product regressions and were packaging gaps.
#
# So: ship what the shipped tests need. Modules that inspect REPOSITORY files
# (project metadata, scripts, module sources) are skipped with an explicit reason
# when those files are absent — see `tests/conftest.py`. Nothing is skipped in a
# real checkout, so CI keeps every gate.

include tests/conftest.py
include tests/fixtures/*.wasm
include tests/fixtures/*.wat

# ...and the two inputs two shipped acceptance modules read at COLLECTION time.
#
# Measured 2026-10-20 on the unpacked 1.2.0 sdist: ten modules errored before a
# single test ran. Nine of them are the repository guards, which read `scripts/`
# at import time and can only ever run in a real checkout (see
# tests/conftest.py); the tenth was the product contract, which the contract
# guard loads at module level. An sdist is a build input, not the test target —
# the wheel is — so the nine stay out of scope and are named in
# docs/release_assurance.md instead of being papered over. The contract and the
# examples ARE shipped: without them those two acceptance modules would turn
# nine collection errors into ten.
include product_contract.json
recursive-include examples *.wasm
# README points at this runnable signed-record demo. Without the source, the
# sdist advertised a companion example that a recipient could not run.
include examples/signed_record_demo.py
